      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment carefully.\n\nThis is part 3 of 3 of an OIML certificate R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 2 (water meters, R49 is for water meters). The fragment contains:\n\n1. Document-level info:\n   - Number: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01, revision 2\n   - Project number: 2254799\n   - Member State: The Netherlands\n   - Pages 7, 8, 9 of 9 → page_total = 9\n   - Issuing authority: NMi (from header \"NMi\")\n\n2. Tables 6-9: General characteristics of the indicating device, organized by:\n   - Table 6: Reduced bore – AC mains powered\n   - Table 7: Reduced bore – Battery / Renewable energy powered\n   - Table 8: Full bore – AC mains powered\n   - Table 9: Full bore – Battery / Renewable energy powered (spans pages 8-9)\n\n3. Production locations (3 locations):\n   - ABB Limited, Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, United Kingdom (UK)\n   - ABB Engineering (Shanghai) Ltd., No. 4528, Kangxin Highway, Pudong New District, Shanghai, 201319, P.R. China\n   - ABB Inc., 125 E. County Line Road, Warminster, PA 18974, United States of America (USA)\n\n4. Certificate history / revision history:\n   - Initial: 14 August 2020 — First issue.\n   - 1: 30 September 2020 — Additional evaluation of reduced bore larger sizes up to DN600\n   - 2: 24 December 2020 — Additional evaluation of (virtual) full bore sensor and additional software version\n\nNow, how should I structure this per the layered schema?\n\n**certificate:**\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\"\n- scheme: \"A\" (from number R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01, the \"-A-\" indicates scheme A)\n- project_number: \"2254799\"\n- page_total: 9\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: revision 2 date is 24 December 2020 → \"2020-12-24\". Hmm, should I infer this? The current revision is 2, and revision 2 is dated 24 December 2020. That's a reasonable inference. But rule 8 says don't fabricate. The date_issued of the current certificate revision could reasonably be taken as the revision 2 date. I'll include it as \"2020-12-24\".\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present, omit.\n\n**issuing_authority:**\n- name: \"NMi\" — that's all we get from this fragment. Include name only.\n\n**applicants / manufacturers:** Not stated in this fragment. Production locations are listed — these aren't manufacturers per se, though ABB is presumably the manufacturer. The production locations list says \"The water meter is produced at one of the following production locations\". These are production locations, not necessarily the applicant/manufacturer listed on page 1. Hmm. Should I put them under manufacturers[]? The schema has manufacturers[] with name and address_lines. Production locations are facilities where the certified type is produced — arguably manufacturer entries. But rule 8 says don't fabricate. The cert fragment doesn't say \"manufacturer\", it says \"production location\". \n\nOptions:\n1. Omit manufacturers entirely.\n2. Include the three ABB entities as manufacturers since they are the production locations of the certified type.\n\nI think including them as manufacturers is reasonable and useful — they're the entities producing the meter. But strictly, the schema is manufacturers[]. Given this is a fragment and the full cert likely has an applicant section on earlier pages, adding these could be duplicative. However, the instruction says extract content present in THIS fragment. The production locations are present here. I'll include them under manufacturers[] since they are production locations of the certified type (that's the closest fit). Actually, hmm — but that could conflict with parts 1 and 2 which presumably have an applicant section. The instruction says \"produce a JSON object following the layered schema for the content present in THIS fragment only.\" So I should extract what's here. Production locations → manufacturers seems the best mapping. I'll include them with a note? No notes field... I could add role. The schema for manufacturers is just name and address_lines. I'll include them as manufacturers.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. Since this is a fragment and merging happens elsewhere presumably, including the production locations as manufacturers is helpful data. I'll do it.\n\n**certified_type:**\n- category: \"Water meter\" (R49 is water meters; the text says \"The water meter is produced at...\"). Include \"Water meter\".\n- type_designations: not present in this fragment (no type names mentioned). Omit.\n- description: not present beyond tables. Could note indicating device characteristics but that's in matrix_tables.\n\n**model_family:** The meter sizes DN40–DN600 form model variants. The tables group sizes. The models here are DN sizes. I could create models: DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600. That's 15 models. That's what the tables cover.\n\n**characteristics:** The tables are config-level: two axes — bore (reduced/full) and power supply (AC mains / battery-renewable energy). For each combination, per meter size: indicating range max, indicating range min, verification scale interval max.\n\nSo config_level entries with two axes? The schema says config_level[]: attribute, axis, values[]: {condition, value}. Axis is one dimension. But here we have two axes: bore and power supply. I could either:\n- Use a combined axis label like \"bore × power_supply\" with conditions like \"reduced_bore, AC_mains\".\n- Or emit multiple config_level entries with combined axis.\n\nI think the cleanest: emit matrix_tables[] for each of the 4 tables (Table 6, 7, 8, 9 plus the unlabeled continuation on page 8 which is the continuation of... wait let me check).\n\nActually, look at page 8: there's a table at the top before Table 9, with header identical. Which table does it belong to? Page 7 ends with Table 8 \"Full bore – AC mains powered\" whose last row is DN200, DN250, DN300. Page 8 starts with a headerless table row: DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600 → 9999999, 9999999, 0,1. Hmm, is this a continuation of Table 8? Table 8 covers DN40–DN300 with 5 rows. Adding DN350–DN600 row would extend Table 8 to DN600. But then Table 9 (Full bore – Battery) starts with DN40, DN50... and includes DN600 at the end. So yes, the page 8 unlabeled table is the continuation of Table 8 (Full bore – AC mains powered). That makes sense: Table 8 full bore AC covers DN40 through DN600.\n\nWait, but Table 8's last VSI was 0,01 for DN200-300, and continuation has 0,1 for DN350-600. Plausible.\n\nSo:\n- Table 6: Reduced bore – AC mains: DN40-600 grouped\n- Table 7: Reduced bore – Battery/Renewable: DN40-600 grouped\n- Table 8: Full bore – AC mains: DN40-DN300 (page 7) + DN350-DN600 (page 8 continuation)\n- Table 9: Full bore – Battery/Renewable: DN40-DN600\n\nValues (converting commas to dots):\n\nTable 6 (Reduced bore, AC mains):\n- DN40, DN50, DN65: max 9999999, min 99999, VSI 0.0001\n- DN80: max 9999999, min 999999, VSI 0.0001\n- DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200: max 9999999, min 999999, VSI 0.001\n- DN250: max 9999999, min 9999999, VSI 0.001\n- DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600: max 9999999, min 9999999, VSI 0.01\n\nTable 7 (Reduced bore, Battery/Renewable):\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- DN65: 9999999, 99999, 0.001\n- DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN200: 9999999, 999999, 0.01\n- DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- DN500, DN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\n\nTable 8 (Full bore, AC mains):\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- DN65: 9999999, 999999, 0.0001\n- DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN200, DN250, DN300: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- (continuation page 8) DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\n\nTable 9 (Full bore, Battery/Renewable):\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.001\n- DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.01\n- DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- DN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\n\nNow how to represent. I think:\n\n1. matrix_tables[]: 4 entries (merging Table 8 with its continuation), each with name, columns, column_units, rows.\n\nColumns: meter_size, indicating_range_max, indicating_range_min, verification_scale_interval_max. Units: null, \"m³\", \"m³\", \"m³\".\n\n2. characteristics.config_level[]: This is the trickier part. Attributes: indicating_range_max, indicating_range_min, verification_scale_interval_max. Axes: bore (reduced|full) and power_supply (AC mains | battery/renewable energy).\n\nSince two axes, I'll use axis: \"bore_and_power_supply\" with conditions like \"reduced_bore / AC mains powered\". Or emit per attribute with axis describing both dims. Let me do:\n\n```json\n\"config_level\": [\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"indicating_range_max\",\n    \"axis\": \"bore × power_supply\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"reduced_bore | AC_mains | DN40-DN65\", \"value\": 9999999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      ...\n    ]\n  },\n  ...\n]\n```\n\nHmm, that could get very long. Alternatively, the condition could be a structured description: \"reduced bore, AC mains, DN40/DN50/DN65\". \n\nActually, since matrix_tables already preserve the tables fully, maybe config_level should be a condensed version. But the rules say to populate model_level/matrix_tables for model families. For config axes, rule 5 says use config_level. I'll populate config_level but keep it manageable.\n\nLet me think about the cleanest representation. For each attribute (3 attributes) × 4 configs, give per-DN values. But many values repeat across DN groups.\n\nOption A: condition = config description + DN size list; value = the value for those sizes.\n\nE.g.:\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"indicating_range_min\",\n  \"axis\": \"meter_size within (bore × power_supply)\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, AC mains, DN40-DN65\", \"value\": 99999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, AC mains, DN80-DN200\", \"value\": 999999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, AC mains, DN250-DN600\", \"value\": 9999999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nWait — Table 6 reduced AC: min is 99999 for DN40-65, 999999 for DN80-200, 9999999 for DN250-600. Yes that grouping works.\n\nTable 7 reduced battery: min 99999 for DN40-65, 999999 for DN80-150... wait DN200 also 999999. So DN80-DN200 → 999999; DN250-DN600 → 9999999.\n\nTable 8 full AC: DN40-50 → 99999; DN65-DN150 → 999999; DN200-DN600 → 9999999.\n\nTable 9 full battery: DN40-50 → 99999; DN65-DN150 → 999999; DN200-DN600 → 9999999.\n\nVSI max values vary more granularly. Table 6: 0.0001 (DN40-80), 0.001 (DN100-250), 0.01 (DN300-600). Table 7: 0.0001 (DN40-50), 0.001 (DN65-150), 0.01 (DN200-450), 0.1 (DN500-600). Table 8: 0.0001 (DN40-65), 0.001 (DN80-150), 0.01 (DN200-300), 0.1 (DN350-600). Table 9: 0.001 (DN40-125), 0.01 (DN150-500), 0.1 (DN600).\n\nThis is getting complex. Given the matrix_tables capture everything precisely, maybe config_level should be included with the full per-DN granularity. Let me just do it fully — conditions like \"reduced bore, AC mains, DN40\" etc. That would be 15 models × 4 configs × 3 attributes = 180 entries. Too verbose.\n\nCompromise: use grouped conditions where values are identical, and represent VSI per group. That's what I sketched above. Let me carefully derive groups.\n\n**Table 6 — Reduced bore, AC mains:**\n\nindicating_range_max: 9999999 for all DN40-DN600. → one condition \"reduced bore, AC mains, DN40-DN600\": 9999999.\n\nindicating_range_min:\n- DN40-DN65: 99999\n- DN80-DN200: 999999\n- DN250-DN600: 9999999\n\nverification_scale_interval_max:\n- DN40-DN80: 0.0001\n- DN100-DN250: 0.001\n- DN300-DN600: 0.01\n\n**Table 7 — Reduced bore, Battery/Renewable energy:**\n\nmax: 9999999 all (DN40-DN600).\n\nmin:\n- DN40-DN65: 99999\n- DN80-DN200: 999999\n- DN250-DN600: 9999999\n\nvsi:\n- DN40-DN50: 0.0001\n- DN65-DN150: 0.001\n- DN200-DN450: 0.01\n- DN500-DN600: 0.1\n\n**Table 8 — Full bore, AC mains:**\n\nmax: 9999999 all (DN40-DN600).\n\nmin:\n- DN40-DN50: 99999\n- DN65-DN150: 999999\n- DN200-DN600: 9999999\n\nvsi:\n- DN40-DN65: 0.0001\n- DN80-DN150: 0.001\n- DN200-DN300: 0.01\n- DN350-DN600: 0.1\n\n**Table 9 — Full bore, Battery/Renewable:**\n\nmax: 9999999 all.\n\nmin:\n- DN40-DN50: 99999\n- DN65-DN150: 999999\n- DN200-DN600: 9999999\n\nvsi:\n- DN40-DN125: 0.001\n- DN150-DN500: 0.01\n- DN600: 0.1\n\nOK so config_level with grouped conditions:\n\n```json\n\"config_level\": [\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"indicating_range_max\",\n    \"axis\": \"bore and power supply (meter size ranges DN40-DN600)\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, AC mains\", \"value\": 9999999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, battery/renewable energy\", \"value\": 9999999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"full bore, AC mains\", \"value\": 9999999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"full bore, battery/renewable energy\", \"value\": 9999999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"}\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"indicating_range_min\",\n    \"axis\": \"meter size within (bore × power supply)\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, AC mains, DN40-DN65\", \"value\": 99999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, AC mains, DN80-DN200\", \"value\": 999999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, AC mains, DN250-DN600\", \"value\": 9999999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, battery/renewable energy, DN40-DN65\", \"value\": 99999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, battery/renewable energy, DN80-DN200\", \"value\": 999999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, battery/renewable energy, DN250-DN600\", \"value\": 9999999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"full bore, AC mains, DN40-DN50\", \"value\": 99999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"full bore, AC mains, DN65-DN150\", \"value\": 999999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"full bore, AC mains, DN200-DN600\", \"value\": 9999999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"full bore, battery/renewable energy, DN40-DN50\", \"value\": 99999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"full bore, battery/renewable energy, DN65-DN150\", \"value\": 999999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"full bore, battery/renewable energy, DN200-DN600\", \"value\": 9999999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"}\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"verification_scale_interval_max\",\n    \"axis\": \"meter size within (bore × power supply)\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, AC mains, DN40-DN80\", \"value\": 0.0001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, AC mains, DN100-DN250\", \"value\": 0.001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, AC mains, DN300-DN600\", \"value\": 0.01, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, battery/renewable energy, DN40-DN50\", \"value\": 0.0001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, battery/renewable energy, DN65-DN150\", \"value\": 0.001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, battery/renewable energy, DN200-DN450\", \"value\": 0.01, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"reduced bore, battery/renewable energy, DN500-DN600\", \"value\": 0.1, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"full bore, AC mains, DN40-DN65\", \"value\": 0.0001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"full bore, AC mains, DN80-DN150\", \"value\": 0.001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"full bore, AC mains, DN200-DN300\", \"value\": 0.01, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"full bore, AC mains, DN350-DN600\", \"value\": 0.1, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"full bore, battery/renewable energy, DN40-DN125\", \"value\": 0.001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"full bore, battery/renewable energy, DN150-DN500\", \"value\": 0.01, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"full bore, battery/renewable energy, DN600\", \"value\": 0.1, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"}\n    ]\n  }\n]\n```\n\nWait, for Table 6 vsi: DN40,DN50,DN65 → 0.0001; DN80 → 0.0001. So DN40-DN80 → 0.0001. DN100,DN125,DN150,DN200 → 0.001; DN250 → 0.001. So DN100-DN250 → 0.001. DN300-DN600 → 0.01. ✓.\n\nTable 7 vsi: DN40,DN50 → 0.0001; DN65 → 0.001; DN80-150 → 0.001; so DN65-DN150 → 0.001. DN200 → 0.01; DN250-450 → 0.01; so DN200-DN450 → 0.01. DN500,600 → 0.1. ✓.\n\nTable 8 vsi: DN40,50 → 0.0001; DN65 → 0.0001; so DN40-DN65 → 0.0001. DN80-150 → 0.001. DN200-300 → 0.01. DN350-600 → 0.1. ✓.\n\nTable 9 vsi: DN40,50 → 0.001; DN65-125 → 0.001; so DN40-DN125 → 0.001. DN150 → 0.01; DN200-500 → 0.01; so DN150-DN500 → 0.01. DN600 → 0.1. ✓.\n\nAlso, should power supply be a type_level characteristic? It's mentioned as config axis. The values \"AC mains\" and \"Battery / Renewable energy\" are configuration options, captured in config_level axis. I could also add a config_level entry for power_supply_type? That's redundant. Skip.\n\n**matrix_tables:** 4 entries. Name them per the document: \"Table 6 General characteristics of the indicating device – Reduced bore – AC mains powered\", etc. Columns: [\"meter_size\", \"indicating_range_max\", \"indicating_range_min\", \"verification_scale_interval_max\"], column_units: [null, \"m³\", \"m³\", \"m³\"].\n\nRows as objects. For Table 8, merge the page 8 continuation row as the last row.\n\n**model_family:** models DN40 ... DN600. 15 models:\nDN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600.\n\nShould I include model_family here? The rule says include only if cert covers >1 model variant. Yes, 15 DN sizes. But this fragment doesn't define the models themselves (they were likely defined in parts 1-2). Still, the tables here enumerate them. Including them helps. I'll include with family_name null and the 15 model_ids. Hmm, but is the family of DN sizes a \"model\" family? The meter size (nominal diameter) is a model variant attribute. Yes, per the R137 example (sizes G1.6/G2.5 → models), DN sizes are analogous. Include.\n\n**recommendation:** id \"R49\". Edition? The cert number says \"R49/2013\" → edition 2013. Should I include? The number embeds 2013. Rule says edition (int). OIML R49:2013 is the edition. The number R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 indicates R 49/2013. I think edition 2013 is safe to include. Hmm, but is it fabrication? The \"2013\" in the certificate number clearly refers to the recommendation edition year per OIML cert numbering convention (R49/2013-A-...). I'll include edition: 2013. accuracy_classes: not present in fragment; omit. scheme: \"A\" — from the cert number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\", the \"A\" after the edition is the scheme. Include scheme \"A\".\n\nWait, careful: the certificate number format is R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01: R49, edition 2013, scheme A, country code NL1, cert 20.01. Yes.\n\n**revision_history:**\n- {revision: \"0\"? No — \"Initial\"} The table says \"Initial\". Rule says revision string e.g. \"0\". The doc says \"Initial\". I'll use \"Initial\" as given? The rule example converts 0 (int) to \"0\" string, not renaming. Here the revision label is \"Initial\" — keep it as \"Initial\". Hmm, or \"0\"? Don't fabricate — use \"Initial\".\n- {revision: \"1\", date: \"2020-09-30\", changes: \"Additional evaluation of reduced bore larger sizes up to DN600\"}\n- {revision: \"2\", date: \"2020-12-24\", changes: \"Additional evaluation of (virtual) full bore sensor and additional software version\"}\n\nDate format: The schema doesn't specify format. Use ISO 8601: \"2020-08-14\", \"2020-09-30\", \"2020-12-24\".\n\n**certificate.date_issued:** The current revision (2) date is 24 December 2020. I'll set date_issued \"2020-12-24\". Reasonable.\n\n**issuing_authority:** name \"NMi\". address/person etc. not in fragment. Just name. Maybe also oiml_issuer_id omit.\n\n**test_reports:** none in fragment. Omit.\n\n**footnotes:** none. Omit.\n\n**components:** none named. Omit. Well, \"indicating device\" is a component... The tables describe the indicating device characteristics. But no type designation given. I could add a component entry role \"indicating device\" without type designations? The schema says include only if cert names sub-assemblies. The cert names \"indicating device\" as the subject of Tables 6-9. Hmm. I could include:\n\n```json\n\"components\": [\n  {\"role\": \"indicating_device\", \"type_designations\": [], \"characteristics\": {...}}\n]\n```\n\nBut the characteristics schema for components isn't deeply defined (\"sub-characteristics\"). The config_level already captures these characteristics at certificate level with axis. I think putting the indicating-device tables in matrix_tables and config_level is enough; adding an empty component adds noise. But naming the component helps context. I'll skip components since no type designations are named — actually, let me reconsider: the component is clearly \"indicating device\". Including `components: [{role: \"indicating device\"}]`... The instruction: \"components[]: include ONLY if cert names sub-assemblies\". It does name one. But without type designations or characteristics slot filled, value is marginal. I'll include it minimally with role and maybe note the tables apply to it? I think I'll include it, with role \"indicating device\" and no other fields... Actually the characteristics{} on component — I could reference config-level characteristics there, but they're already in characteristics.config_level. Duplication is bad. I'll omit components to avoid noise. Hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider once more. The fragment's tables are titled \"General characteristics of the indicating device\". If downstream merging combines parts 1-3, the indicating device characteristics belong with the component. But since this output is for the fragment only, and the schema's characteristics.config_level is at certificate level, keeping them there is fine. I'll omit components[] — no, wait. I think including it adds provenance clarity: role \"indicating_device\", characteristics referencing... I'll keep it simple and omit. Hmm, but the grader might expect components for indicating device. The schema example roles: \"Measuring sensor FM01\", \"Pulser P2\". Those have type designations. Here none. I'll omit.\n\n**certified_type.category:** \"Water meter\". Confirmed by text \"The water meter is produced at...\".\n\n**certified_type.description:** Could add: \"Water meter with indicating device; characteristics of the indicating device given per meter size, bore type and power supply.\" That's derived, mild fabrication risk. I'll include a brief description? Rule 8 says don't fabricate. A concise description from the table titles is OK-ish. I'll skip description or keep minimal. I'll skip.\n\n**production locations:** manufacturers[]:\n1. ABB Limited — [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom (UK)\"]\n2. ABB Engineering (Shanghai) Ltd. — [\"No. 4528, Kangxin Highway\", \"Pudong New District\", \"Shanghai, 201319\", \"P.R. China\"]\n3. ABB Inc. — [\"125 E. County Line Road\", \"Warminster, PA 18974\", \"United States of America (USA)\"]\n\nInclude these as manufacturers. \n\nShould certificate.section_pages or anything? Not in schema. Skip.\n\nAlso the fragment repeats header \"Number R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 2\". certificate.number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\". revision is captured in revision_history; there's no certificate.revision field in schema. Could add \"revision\": \"2\"? Schema doesn't list it. Rule 8: omit fields not present... but adding non-schema fields? Better to stick to schema. The revision info is in revision_history anyway